 "drivingQuestion": "How can a price be set on potter's clumps under Rav's view?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Already rolled and dried; buyer removes by custom",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_metzia.74a.12",
   "daf": "74a",
   "sentenceId": 11,
   "he": "וְעַל הַבֵּיצִים שֶׁל יוֹצֵר. אַמַּאי? וְהָא מְחוּסָּר לָפוֹפֵי וְיַבּוֹשֵׁי, עַיּוֹלֵי לְאַתּוּנָא לְמִשְׂרַף וּלְמִיפַּק! כְּגוֹן דִּמְלָפְפָה וִיבִישׁוּ. וְהָא אִיכָּא תְּלָת! בְּאַתְרָא דְּהָהוּא דְּזָבֵין הוּא דְּמַפֵּיק.",
   "en": "The mishna teaches that one may set a price «for the clumps» of clay prepared for use «by a potter.» Based on this, the Gemara challenges the opinion of Rav: «Why» is this permitted? «But» there are still several actions that are «lacking: Rolling» them out to the proper size, «drying» them, «putting» them «into the kiln, burning» them, «and removing» them from the kiln. The Gemara answers: The mishna is discussing a case «where they were» already «rolled and dried.» The Gemara challenges: «But there are three» actions that are lacking. The Gemara explains: The mishna is discussing «a place where» the local custom is that «the one who purchases» the clumps of clay is «the one who removes» them from the kiln."
  }
 ],
